<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=julian-calendar">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julian calendar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Julian calendar is a solar calendar introduced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in 46 BCE, with a leap year every four years. It remained the standard calendar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> until the Gregorian reform. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalali-calendar">Jalali calendar</a> devised under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> was more accurate than the Julian calendar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julian-calendar.jpg" alt="Julian calendar" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fasti_Praenestini_Massimo_n2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Julian calendar was proposed in 46 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, from whom it takes its name, as a reform of the earlier Roman calendar, which was largely lunisolar. That calendar had 12 months and 355 days, with a 27- or 28-day intercalary month inserted between February and March in some years. The reform took effect on 1 January 45 BCE by Caesar's edict. To realign the year with the seasons, 46 BCE was made 445 days long: it already had 378 days through the regular intercalary month, and Caesar added 67 more by inserting two extraordinary intercalary months between November and December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The calendar has normal years of 365 days and leap years of 366, in a cycle of three normal years and one leap year that repeats without exception, giving an average year of 365.25 days. The months were formed by adding ten days to the 355-day year: two extra days went to January, Sextilis and December, and one each to April, June, September and November. February stayed at 28 days, so the ordinary month lengths are those still used today.</p><p>The old intercalary month was abolished and replaced by a single leap day at the same point, dated the sixth doubled day before the Kalends of March, which gave English the word bissextile. The pontifices at first added a leap day every three years instead of every four, an error recorded by several ancient writers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Caesar's reform applied only to the Roman calendar, but in the following decades many civic and provincial calendars of the empire were aligned to it, and it became the predominant calendar of the Western world for more than 1,600 years. Because the Julian year is longer than the solar year, the calendar gains one day every 128 years. By 1582, 21 March was ten days out of alignment with the equinox, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xiii">Pope Gregory XIII</a> promulgated a revised calendar.</p><p>Most Catholic countries adopted it at once and Protestant countries over the following two centuries; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> was the last to adopt it as its civil calendar, in 1923. Most Eastern Orthodox churches still base their liturgical year on the Julian calendar, as does the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> calendar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Julian calendar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Caesar’s reform of the calendar in 46 BCE produced the <b>Julian calendar</b>, the basis of the one used today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…after well over a million military dead and severe supply failures, bread riots in Petrograd turned into revolution when the garrison mutinied, and with his generals urging abdication, Nicholas II gave up the throne on 15 March 1917 (2 March in the <b>Julian calendar</b> then used in Russia).</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The poet and mathematician Omar Khayyam worked under Seljuk patronage on the Jalali solar calendar of 1079, more accurate than the <b>Julian calendar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Part of the Eastern Christian churches keep Christmas on 25 December of the <b>Julian calendar</b>, which currently corresponds to 7 January, and the Armenian Christmas falls on 6 January, the traditional day of Epiphany.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Part of the Eastern churches keep the feast on 25 December of the <b>Julian calendar</b>, which currently corresponds to 7 January, and the Iranian solstice night bears a name borrowed from Syriac-speaking Christians, whose word for birth it is.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>Arnošt of Pardubice, an advisor and diplomat to Charles IV, was confirmed bishop of Prague (3 March by the <b>Julian calendar</b>).</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-11">11 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
